course: dictionary
lesson: 3110
title: One word, two jobs: elbow
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words are both a thing and an action. Elbow is a noun when it names a body part: «The funny bone is not a bone but a nerve running over the elbow close to the skin.» Elbow is a verb when someone does something with it: «He pins the driver down with his elbow.» Look at the words around the word to see which job it is doing.
explain: Context tells you the meaning. In «He pins the driver down with his elbow», the word with shows that the elbow is a tool being used, so elbow is acting like a verb. In «A sneeze can spray droplets several metres, so the advice is to catch it in a tissue», the word is about the body, so you do not need a verb meaning here.
explain: Elbow can also appear in phrases that do not mean a bent arm at all. «Elbow room» means space to move, and to elbow your way through a crowd means to push rudely past people. Check the sentence: does it make sense if you swap in «pushed» or «space»? If it does, you have the right meaning.
fact: Elbow as a noun means the joint in the middle of your arm where it bends.
fact: Elbow as a verb means to push someone with your elbow.
fact: The elbow is a hinge joint, which lets the arm bend one way, like a door.
fact: «Elbow room» means space to move, and «elbow your way» means to push rudely through a crowd.
fact: Words that look the same can have different jobs: elbow is a noun in «an elbow» and a verb in «to elbow someone».
exercise: What is the elbow? | The space between your toes | The top of your head | *The joint in the middle of your arm, where it bends | The bone in your little finger
exercise: «He elbowed his way through the crowd.» What does elbowed his way mean? | *Pushed rudely through | Waved to his friends | Ran slowly round the edge | Sat down quietly
exercise: «Keep your elbows off the table.» What kind of rule is this? | A maths rule | A rule about football only | A weather rule | *A manners rule
test: What kind of joint is the elbow, like a door? | *Hinge | Pivot | Ball-and-socket | Gliding
test: Why should you sneeze into your elbow? | To look polite in front of friends | To keep your arm warm | *To stop germs spreading to your hands | To make your sneeze louder
exam: In «Elbow room means space to move», what does elbow room mean? | A room with a sink | Running very fast | *Space to move | Bending your arm
